july 2021 was the hottest month in  modern history and few places were  hotter than the middle east and north  africa where sea and land temperatures  neared or hit new records here in the  gulf we're used to scorching heat with  temperatures that regularly top 45  degrees celsius 113 degrees fahrenheit  scientists like george zitis say this  heat will only get more extreme we have  very strong amounts of solar radiation  very low cloud cover and precipitation  this is causing the extreme  environmental conditions that we see in  the region  and we expect this to become worse in  the future data studied by zitis and his  team indicate that business as usual in  greenhouse gas emissions will soon cause  unprecedented heat waves in the  mediterranean a heat and a decline in  precipitation could drive more intense  droughts like the ones that created the  conditions for wildfires in places like  greece turkey and algeria this summer in  the gulf heat waves could top 56 degrees  celsius and last for weeks  actually we're expecting to reach the  limits of survivability in parts of the  region so human discomfort will be  really high we expect to have direct  impacts for example heat strokes heat  tolerant animals could be affected  coastal cities in the persian gulf like  dubai and daha have already felt  conditions close to these limits humans  regulate their body temperature by  sweating but at high enough levels of  heat and humidity a so-called wet bulb  temperature sweat won't evaporate fast  enough for humans to keep body heat in  check for more than a few hours  air conditioning is mostly ubiquitous in  the gulf but temperature extremes pose a  new challenge when the dry bulb and wet  build temperature increases the humidity  goes up and these big cooling towers  which are responsible for giving us the  cold air inside would not be enough to  give you a comfortable temperature  inside the house flashy plans for  futuristic sustainable cities like neom  and saudi arabia may someday allow for  more efficient living in the gulf  but for now inefficient air conditioning  and energy subsidies are two of the  reasons these countries are among the  world's worst polluters we did some  analysis and we found that one degree  increase in the temperature  outdoor  will increase by 10 percent the air  conditioning energy use in riyadh but  there is limited initiative at least the  existing what we call the old build  retrofit and actually this is not a  problem for the gulf that's throughout  the world but there's a potential upside  jobs we estimate about a quarter of a  million per year over 10 years in saudi  arabia we mitigate climate change we  reduce the amounts of greenhouse gases  in the atmosphere  the temperature will still increase but  in a much slower pace and this will  provide us the window that we need to  adapt in doha simone foxman bloomberg  news
 

